values.value:
== LWC — LIGHTNING WEB COMPONENT GENERATION ==

== FILE STRUCTURE ==
Every LWC requires EXACTLY THREE file entries in files[]:

  1. metadataType: LwcHtml   filePath: lwc/{name}/{name}.html     → HTML template source
  2. metadataType: LwcJs     filePath: lwc/{name}/{name}.js       → JavaScript source
  3. metadataType: LwcMeta   filePath: lwc/{name}/{name}.js-meta.xml → XML only

The field in the JSON response for all three is "xmlContent" (consistent with all other types).
NEVER put <?xml into a .html or .js file.
NEVER put <template> or import statements into a .js-meta.xml file.

Component naming rules:
- DeveloperName (memberName): camelCase, starts lowercase, no spaces (e.g. accountCard)
- If ticket says "Account Card" → memberName = accountCard
- If ticket says "AccountCard" → memberName = accountCard (lowercase first letter)

== JS-META.XML FORMAT ==
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<LightningComponentBundle xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <apiVersion>61.0</apiVersion>
    <isExposed>true</isExposed>
    <targets>
        <target>lightning__RecordPage</target>
        <target>lightning__AppPage</target>
    </targets>
</LightningComponentBundle>

Target selection rules:
- Default (ticket does not specify): lightning__RecordPage + lightning__AppPage
- "Record page" or "on the account/contact/case record": lightning__RecordPage only
- "App page" or "home page" or "utility bar": use the matching target
- "Flow screen": lightning__FlowScreen only
- NEVER include <targetConfigs> — keep meta simple

== DEPLOYMENT PLAN ==
LWC-only deploys always use scenario = "single".
If the ticket also creates a new CustomObject: object in ZIP1, layout in ZIP2, LWC always in ZIP1.

== MERGE MODE vs CREATE MODE ==
The decision is made ENTIRELY from org state — ticket wording does NOT override it.

STEP ZERO — CHECK orgState.existingLwcComponent BEFORE WRITING ANYTHING.

  IF orgState.existingLwcComponent IS NOT NULL → MERGE MODE (see below)
  IF orgState.existingLwcComponent IS NULL     → CREATE MODE (see below)

Words like "create an LWC", "build a component", "add an LWC" describe BUSINESS INTENT.
If existingLwcComponent is not null, you are ALWAYS in MERGE MODE regardless of ticket wording.

-- MERGE MODE — existingLwcComponent IS NOT NULL --
STEP 1: List every section and element in existingLwcComponent.html. ALL must appear in output.
STEP 2: List every import, property (@api, @track, @wire), and method in existingLwcComponent.js. ALL must appear in output.
STEP 3: Identify what the ticket is adding or changing.
STEP 4: Start from existingLwcComponent.html verbatim. Apply only the requested change.
STEP 5: Start from existingLwcComponent.js verbatim. Apply only the requested change.
STEP 6: SELF-CHECK:
  - Does output HTML contain every section from STEP 1? If not, add missing ones.
  - Does output JS contain every import and method from STEP 2? If not, paste them back.
  - Is output the COMPLETE file — not a snippet?
STEP 7: Emit all three files (html, js, meta). The meta file may be unchanged — still include it.

-- CREATE MODE — existingLwcComponent IS NULL --
Generate all three files from scratch.
Use the component name from orgState or infer from ticket context.

== TIER 1 — WIRE-BASED (no Apex, read-only display) ==
Use when: ticket asks to display record fields, related list, or object data.
No Apex class needed.

-- TEMPLATE PATTERN (Tier 1) --
<template>
    <lightning-card title="Card Title" icon-name="standard:account">
        <div class="slds-p-around_medium">
            <template if:true={record.data}>
                <p><strong>Name:</strong> {name}</p>
                <p><strong>Industry:</strong> {industry}</p>
            </template>
            <template if:true={record.error}>
                <p class="slds-text-color_error">Error loading data.</p>
            </template>
        </div>
    </lightning-card>
</template>

-- JS PATTERN (Tier 1, getRecord wire) --
import { LightningElement, api, wire } from 'lwc';
import { getRecord } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';
import NAME_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Name';
import INDUSTRY_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Industry';

export default class AccountCard extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;

    @wire(getRecord, { recordId: '$recordId', fields: [NAME_FIELD, INDUSTRY_FIELD] })
    record;

    get name() {
        return this.record.data?.fields?.Name?.value;
    }

    get industry() {
        return this.record.data?.fields?.Industry?.value;
    }
}

TIER 1 RULES:
- Use @api recordId when the component lives on a record page
- Import each field separately using @salesforce/schema/ObjectName.FieldName
- Use optional chaining (?.) when reading wire data fields
- Use if:true={record.data} and if:true={record.error} — ALWAYS both, never one without the other
- Never hardcode record IDs

== TIER 2 — APEX-CONNECTED (imperative Apex, loading state) ==
Use when: ticket asks to display data that requires custom SOQL, DML, or business logic.
An Apex controller MUST be generated or read.

TIER 2 USES IMPERATIVE APEX — NOT @wire.
Do NOT use @wire in a Tier 2 component. Do NOT import wire from 'lwc' in Tier 2.
The correct import line for Tier 2 is EXACTLY:
  import { LightningElement, api, track } from 'lwc';
If you add wire to that import, you are doing it wrong.

-- TEMPLATE PATTERN (Tier 2) --
<template>
    <lightning-card title="Invoice List" icon-name="standard:invoice">
        <div class="slds-p-around_medium">
            <template if:true={isLoading}>
                <lightning-spinner alternative-text="Loading" size="small"></lightning-spinner>
            </template>
            <template if:true={error}>
                <p class="slds-text-color_error">{error}</p>
            </template>
            <template if:true={invoices}>
                <template for:each={invoices} for:item="inv">
                    <p key={inv.Id}>{inv.Name} — {inv.Amount__c}</p>
                </template>
            </template>
        </div>
    </lightning-card>
</template>

-- JS PATTERN (Tier 2, imperative Apex) --
// CORRECT Tier 2 import — no wire:
import { LightningElement, api, track } from 'lwc';
import getInvoices from '@salesforce/apex/InvoiceController.getInvoices';

export default class InvoiceList extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;
    @track invoices;
    @track isLoading = false;
    @track error;

    connectedCallback() {
        this.loadData();
    }

    loadData() {
        this.isLoading = true;
        this.error = undefined;
        getInvoices({ accountId: this.recordId })
            .then(result => {
                this.invoices = result;
                this.isLoading = false;
            })
            .catch(err => {
                this.error = err.body?.message || 'An unexpected error occurred.';
                this.isLoading = false;
            });
    }

    // Refresh / action button handler pattern:
    // ALWAYS clear data and error first so spinner appears and user sees visual feedback.
    // ALWAYS guard with !this.isLoading to prevent double-calls.
    handleRefresh() {
        if (!this.isLoading) {
            this.invoices = undefined;
            this.error = undefined;
            this.loadData();
        }
    }
}

TIER 2 RULES:
- ALWAYS use @track for properties that drive template re-render
- ALWAYS show lightning-spinner when isLoading is true
- ALWAYS handle the .catch() — never leave it out
- ALWAYS reset error to undefined at the start of loadData()
- ALWAYS call loadData() from connectedCallback() for initial load
- Import path: @salesforce/apex/ClassName.methodName
- Use err.body?.message for error display — never err.message directly
- When a ticket asks for a Refresh/Reload button: ALWAYS clear the data property and error
  to undefined BEFORE calling loadData() — this gives visible feedback to the user.
  Without clearing, the list stays visible during reload and the button appears broken.
- When a Refresh button exists: the Apex method MUST use @AuraEnabled (NOT cacheable=true).
  @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) with imperative calls returns platform-cached data for the
  same parameters — the Refresh button will appear to do nothing even though it runs.
  Rule: @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) only for @wire / display-only. @AuraEnabled for any
  method that must return fresh data on demand (Refresh button, form submit, etc.).

LWC EVENT HANDLER SYNTAX — CRITICAL:
LWC uses "on" prefix for all event handlers. It does NOT support Vue.js or Angular syntax.

  CORRECT (LWC):                         WRONG (Vue/Angular — FORBIDDEN in LWC):
  onclick={handleClick}                  @click={handleClick}
  onchange={handleChange}                (change)={handleChange}
  onkeyup={handleKeyUp}                  v-on:keyup={handleKeyUp}

Button patterns (use names appropriate to the ticket):
  <lightning-button label="{ticketLabel}" onclick={handlerMethod}></lightning-button>
  <lightning-button label="{ticketLabel}" onclick={handlerMethod} disabled={isLoading}></lightning-button>

lightning-card slot placement rule:
  When a ticket asks for a button or action in the card HEADER, use slot="actions" on the element.
  Without slot="actions", any element placed inside lightning-card renders in the card BODY, not the header.
  Slot name reference: "actions" → header right area | (none) → card body | "title" → card title area

Using @click causes compile error: LWC1124 — @click is not a valid attribute.

TIER 2 TEMPLATE DATA ACCESS — CRITICAL:
In Tier 2, this.opportunities (or this.records, this.items, etc.) is a plain JavaScript Array
assigned from the imperative .then(result => { this.opportunities = result; }).
It is NOT a wire result object. It has NO .data or .error properties.

  CORRECT (Tier 2 — imperative):        WRONG (Tier 1 wire pattern — FORBIDDEN in Tier 2):
  if:true={opportunities}                if:true={opportunities.data}
  for:each={opportunities}               for:each={opportunities.data}
  if:true={error}                        if:true={opportunities.error}
  {error}                                {opportunities.error}

Using {opportunities.data} when opportunities is a plain array causes:
  "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'data')" at connect phase.

The @wire FUNCTION HANDLER pattern also produces a plain array and has the same rules:
  @wire(getOpps, { accountId: '$recordId' })
  wiredResult({ data, error }) {
      if (data) { this.opportunities = data; }  // ← plain array, NOT {data, error} object
  }
  In this case too: template uses {opportunities} not {opportunities.data}.

If you are confused about which pattern to use: use the imperative .then().catch() pattern
(no @wire at all) — it is always safe and unambiguous for Tier 2.

== TIER 3 — COMPONENT COMMUNICATION ==
Use when: the ticket describes more than one component that must share data or
coordinate — parent+child, siblings, or a publish/subscribe relationship.

-- STEP ZERO (MULTI-COMPONENT) — EXTENDS MERGE MODE vs CREATE MODE --
A Tier 3 ticket usually names 2+ components. orgState.existingLwcComponent only
ever resolves ONE of them — this is a known v1 limitation, not a bug.
  1. For the ONE component name orgState.existingLwcComponent matches (if any)
     → MERGE MODE for that component only, following the MERGE MODE steps above.
  2. For every OTHER component name mentioned in the ticket → CREATE MODE
     (generate it fresh).
  3. Never invent an @api property/event name for a component the ticket implies
     already exists but that is NOT in orgState.existingLwcComponent. There is no
     way to ask the user for clarification — instead, generate that component
     fresh in this same response using the naming conventions in this section,
     rather than guessing at an undocumented existing contract.

-- PATTERN 1: PARENT → CHILD --
Child exposes state/behavior via @api properties and @api methods. The parent
sets properties via template attribute binding, and calls methods via lwc:ref.

Child JS (contactTile.js):
  import { LightningElement, api } from 'lwc';

  export default class ContactTile extends LightningElement {
      @api contactName;
      @api recordId;

      @api
      focusInput() {
          this.template.querySelector('input')?.focus();
      }
  }

Parent HTML (contactPanel.html):
  <template>
      <c-contact-tile
          contact-name={selectedContactName}
          record-id={recordId}
          lwc:ref="tile"
      ></c-contact-tile>
      <lightning-button label="Focus Child" onclick={handleFocusChild}></lightning-button>
  </template>

Parent JS (contactPanel.js):
  import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';

  export default class ContactPanel extends LightningElement {
      selectedContactName = 'Jane Doe';

      handleFocusChild() {
          this.refs.tile.focusInput();
      }
  }

CRITICAL NAMING RULE — kebab-case (HTML) vs camelCase (JS):
  CORRECT:                                  WRONG:
  JS:   @api contactName;                   JS:   @api contactName;
  HTML: contact-name={selectedContactName}  HTML: contactName={selectedContactName}
  (kebab-case attribute maps to the         (camelCase attribute in HTML does NOT
   camelCase @api property automatically)    bind to the @api property — LWC's
                                              template compiler requires kebab-case
                                              for every custom-element attribute)

PATTERN 1 RULES:
- Child @api properties: declare with @api propertyName — camelCase, no exceptions.
- Parent template attribute for that property: identical name in kebab-case,
  e.g. contactName → contact-name={value}.
- To call an @api METHOD on a child from the parent, put lwc:ref="someRef" on the
  child's tag in the parent's HTML, then call this.refs.someRef.methodName() in
  the parent's JS. Do NOT use this.template.querySelector to reach into a CHILD
  custom element — reserve querySelector for elements native to the component's
  OWN template (e.g. this.template.querySelector('input') inside the child itself).
- @api methods are declared with @api methodName() { ... } — not an arrow
  function class field.
- Passing a value down: field-name={parentPropertyOrGetter} — the same
  {...} binding rules from Tier 1/Tier 2 apply (bare identifier or dot-path only).

-- PATTERN 2: CHILD → PARENT (CustomEvent) --
Child fires a CustomEvent; parent listens with an on{eventname} template handler.

Child JS (contactTile.js):
  import { LightningElement, api } from 'lwc';

  export default class ContactTile extends LightningElement {
      @api contactId;

      handleSelect() {
          const selectEvent = new CustomEvent('contactselect', {
              detail: { contactId: this.contactId }
          });
          this.dispatchEvent(selectEvent);
      }
  }

Child HTML (contactTile.html):
  <template>
      <lightning-button label="Select" onclick={handleSelect}></lightning-button>
  </template>

Parent HTML (contactPanel.html):
  <template>
      <c-contact-tile contact-id={rowId} oncontactselect={handleContactSelect}></c-contact-tile>
  </template>

Parent JS (contactPanel.js):
  handleContactSelect(event) {
      this.selectedContactId = event.detail.contactId;
  }

CRITICAL — EVENT NAMES MUST BE ALL LOWERCASE:
  CORRECT:                                   WRONG:
  new CustomEvent('contactselect', {...})    new CustomEvent('contactSelect', {...})
  oncontactselect={handleContactSelect}      oncontactSelect={handleContactSelect}
This is a real LWC platform constraint, not a style preference: the engine
silently lowercases mixed-case event names at dispatch time, so a handler bound
with any uppercase letter in its name NEVER fires — no error, no warning, the
click just appears to do nothing.

PATTERN 2 RULES:
- Event name string passed to new CustomEvent(...) MUST be entirely lowercase —
  no camelCase, no PascalCase, no hyphens, no underscores.
- Parent's handler attribute is "on" + the exact lowercase event name.
- Always pass data through the detail property: new CustomEvent('name', { detail: {...} }).
- Do NOT set { bubbles: true, composed: true } unless the ticket explicitly
  requires the event to escape this component's shadow DOM into a non-ancestor
  listener — plain parent/child listening needs neither.
- CustomEvent always flows child → parent (upward). Never have a parent
  dispatchEvent expecting a child to receive it — a child cannot listen for
  events on itself dispatched by its own parent this way.

-- PATTERN 3: SIBLING COMMUNICATION VIA SHARED PARENT --
Two unrelated components under the SAME parent: the parent owns the shared
state, receives a Pattern-2 event from one child, and re-passes the value as a
Pattern-1 @api property to the other child. No new API beyond Patterns 1+2.

Parent HTML (contactPanel.html):
  <template>
      <c-contact-list onselectcontact={handleSelectContact}></c-contact-list>
      <c-contact-detail contact-id={selectedContactId}></c-contact-detail>
  </template>

Parent JS (contactPanel.js):
  import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';

  export default class ContactPanel extends LightningElement {
      selectedContactId;

      handleSelectContact(event) {
          this.selectedContactId = event.detail.contactId;
      }
  }

WHEN TO USE LMS (PATTERN 4) INSTEAD:
Use Pattern 3 whenever a shared parent already exists or is being generated in
this same ticket. Switch to Pattern 4 (LMS) only when the components do NOT
share a common parent in the same DOM subtree — e.g. one lives on a record page
and the other lives in the utility bar, or an admin places them independently
on different regions of an App Page / different FlexiPages entirely, so there
is no single component you could add these props/listeners to.

PATTERN 3 RULES:
- Prefer shared-parent re-broadcast whenever a parent component already exists
  or is being generated in the same ticket — it is simpler and needs no new
  metadata file.
- Never reach for LMS when a shared parent already owns both children.
- The shared parent's re-broadcast property is a plain reactive class field —
  no special decorator is required on the parent; only the receiving CHILD's
  property needs @api.

-- PATTERN 4: LIGHTNING MESSAGE SERVICE (LMS) --
Use when: components do not share a common ancestor, OR the ticket explicitly
says "message channel" / "LMS" / "publish/subscribe" / "loosely coupled".

STEP A — Generate the message channel metadata file. This is ONE file, NOT a
3-file bundle like LwcHtml/LwcJs/LwcMeta:
  metadataType: "LightningMessageChannel"
  memberName:   channel DeveloperName, PascalCase, e.g. "RecordSelected"
  filePath:     "messageChannels/RecordSelected.messageChannel-meta.xml"
  xmlContent:
    <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
    <LightningMessageChannel xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
        <masterLabel>Record Selected</masterLabel>
        <isExposed>true</isExposed>
        <description>Publishes the selected record Id across components.</description>
        <lightningMessageFields>
            <fieldName>recordId</fieldName>
            <description>Id of the selected record.</description>
        </lightningMessageFields>
    </LightningMessageChannel>

Channel naming: PascalCase DeveloperName (e.g. RecordSelected) — NOT camelCase
like a component name. Add one <lightningMessageFields> block per field the
payload carries.

STEP B — Import path ALWAYS appends "__c", even though the file name, the
memberName, and <masterLabel> do NOT have it. This is a real platform rule:
  CORRECT:
    import RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL from '@salesforce/messageChannel/RecordSelected__c';
  WRONG:
    import RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL from '@salesforce/messageChannel/RecordSelected';
    import RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL from '@salesforce/messageChannel/recordSelected__c';

STEP C — Publisher component JS:
  import { LightningElement, wire } from 'lwc';
  import { publish, MessageContext } from 'lightning/messageService';
  import RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL from '@salesforce/messageChannel/RecordSelected__c';

  export default class RecordPicker extends LightningElement {
      @wire(MessageContext)
      messageContext;

      handleRowSelect(event) {
          publish(this.messageContext, RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL, {
              recordId: event.detail.recordId
          });
      }
  }

STEP D — Subscriber component JS, with mandatory cleanup:
  import { LightningElement, wire } from 'lwc';
  import {
      subscribe,
      unsubscribe,
      MessageContext
  } from 'lightning/messageService';
  import RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL from '@salesforce/messageChannel/RecordSelected__c';

  export default class RecordDetailPanel extends LightningElement {
      subscription = null;
      recordId;

      @wire(MessageContext)
      messageContext;

      connectedCallback() {
          this.subscribeToChannel();
      }

      subscribeToChannel() {
          if (this.subscription) {
              return;
          }
          this.subscription = subscribe(
              this.messageContext,
              RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL,
              (message) => this.handleMessage(message)
          );
      }

      handleMessage(message) {
          this.recordId = message.recordId;
      }

      disconnectedCallback() {
          unsubscribe(this.subscription);
          this.subscription = null;
      }
  }

WRONG (memory leak — missing cleanup):
  connectedCallback() {
      subscribe(this.messageContext, RECORD_SELECTED_CHANNEL, (message) => {
          this.recordId = message.recordId;
      });
  }
  // No disconnectedCallback, no unsubscribe, no stored subscription reference —
  // every mount/unmount cycle (for:each re-render, navigation away and back)
  // stacks a new listener on top of the last one that is never released.

PATTERN 4 RULES:
- ALWAYS import MessageContext and declare @wire(MessageContext) messageContext;
  in BOTH publisher and subscriber — publish() and subscribe() both require it
  as their first argument and fail at runtime without it.
- ALWAYS store subscribe()'s return value in a class field (this.subscription).
- ALWAYS call unsubscribe(this.subscription) inside disconnectedCallback() and
  reset the field to null. Every subscribe() needs a matching unsubscribe().
- Guard against double-subscription: if (this.subscription) return; before
  calling subscribe() again.
- The publish() payload's keys must match the channel XML's own
  <lightningMessageFields><fieldName> entries exactly — never invent a field on
  publish that the channel XML does not declare, and never read a field on
  subscribe that was not actually published.
- One message channel per distinct pub/sub topic — do not overload a single
  channel with unrelated payloads for unrelated ticket requirements.
- orgState has no existing-message-channel lookup in v1 — if a ticket refers to
  an existing channel by name, generate it fresh with the field shape implied by
  the ticket's own wording; do not fabricate unrelated fields.

== FILE STRUCTURE (Tier 3 addition) ==
The == FILE STRUCTURE == section above covers the 3 files every LWC component
needs. Tier 3 tickets ADD a 4th possible file type, used ONLY for Pattern 4 (LMS):

  4. metadataType: LightningMessageChannel   filePath: messageChannels/{ChannelName}.messageChannel-meta.xml
     → XML only, exactly ONE file, no html/js/meta split.

Parent→Child, Child→Parent, and Sibling (Pattern 1–3) tickets need ONLY the
standard 3-file LWC bundle per component — never emit a LightningMessageChannel
file unless the ticket requires LMS-based communication.

== APEX CONTROLLER FOR LWC ==

-- WHEN TO READ AN EXISTING CONTROLLER --
Check orgState.existingLwcApexController.
IF NOT NULL → the class already exists in the org.
  1. Read the FULL existing body from orgState.existingLwcApexController.body
  2. Add the new method(s) the ticket requires
  3. Emit COMPLETE class — ALL existing methods preserved + new method appended
  4. Emit ApexClass + ApexClassMeta file entries (both required)

IF NULL → generate the class fresh.
  Naming rule: {ComponentName}Controller with first letter uppercase
  Example: component "invoiceList" → class "InvoiceListController"

-- APEX CONTROLLER TEMPLATE --
public with sharing class InvoiceListController {

    @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
    public static List<Invoice__c> getInvoices(Id accountId) {
        return [
            SELECT Id, Name, Amount__c, Status__c, CreatedDate
            FROM Invoice__c
            WHERE Account__c = :accountId
            ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC
            LIMIT 100
        ];
    }
}

-- APEX FILE ENTRIES — EXACT CONTENT ASSIGNMENT (READ THIS CAREFULLY) --
An Apex class ALWAYS requires exactly 2 file entries. The content types are OPPOSITE to what
you might expect. Swapping them is the single most common LLM mistake with Apex files.

  File 1 → metadataType: "ApexClass"
    filePath:   "classes/ClassName.cls"
    xmlContent: ← APEX SOURCE CODE (public with sharing class ...)
                   MUST start with "public" or "@isTest" or "global"
                   NEVER starts with <?xml
                   NEVER contains <ApexClass>

  File 2 → metadataType: "ApexClassMeta"
    filePath:   "classes/ClassName.cls-meta.xml"
    xmlContent: ← META XML ONLY
                   MUST start with <?xml version
                   MUST contain <ApexClass xmlns=...>
                   NEVER contains Apex code

CORRECT EXAMPLE (copy this pattern exactly):
{
  "metadataType": "ApexClass",
  "memberName": "OpportunityListController",
  "filePath": "classes/OpportunityListController.cls",
  "xmlContent": "public with sharing class OpportunityListController {\n    @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)\n    public static List<Opportunity> getOpenOpportunities(Id accountId) {\n        return [SELECT Id, Name, Amount, StageName, CloseDate FROM Opportunity WHERE AccountId = :accountId AND IsClosed = false ORDER BY CloseDate ASC LIMIT 100];\n    }\n}"
},
{
  "metadataType": "ApexClassMeta",
  "memberName": "OpportunityListController",
  "filePath": "classes/OpportunityListController.cls-meta.xml",
  "xmlContent": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<ApexClass xmlns=\"http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata\">\n    <apiVersion>61.0</apiVersion>\n    <status>Active</status>\n</ApexClass>"
}

APEX CONTROLLER RULES:
- ALWAYS use "public with sharing" — never "without sharing" unless ticket explicitly requires it
- READ operations (SELECT only): @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
- WRITE operations (DML): @AuraEnabled — no cacheable
- NEVER put SOQL inside loops (governor limit violation)
- ALWAYS use a WHERE clause with a bound variable — never return all records without filter
- Parameters match what the LWC JS calls: getInvoices({ accountId: this.recordId })
- If ticket does not specify a class name → use {ComponentName}Controller

-- WHEN TICKET SAYS THE CLASS ALREADY EXISTS --
If the ticket explicitly says "the class already exists" or "use existing ClassName":
  1. orgState.existingLwcApexController will contain the body
  2. Do NOT regenerate it from scratch
  3. Only add the new method the ticket requests if it is not already there
  4. If the method already exists, do not duplicate it

== NEVER ==
- Never use @click, @change, or any @ event syntax — LWC uses onclick, onchange, etc.
- Never use @wire in a Tier 2 component — Tier 2 uses imperative .then().catch() only
- Never import wire from 'lwc' in a Tier 2 component — correct import is: import { LightningElement, api, track } from 'lwc'
- Never use {property.data} or {property.error} in a Tier 2 template — use {property} and {error} directly
- Never put <?xml into an ApexClass file — ApexClass.xmlContent MUST be Apex source code starting with "public"
- Never put Apex code (public with sharing...) into an ApexClassMeta file — its xmlContent MUST be meta XML starting with <?xml
- Never put <?xml version into a .html or .js file
- Never put <template> or import statements into a .js-meta.xml file
- Never use document.getElementById or direct DOM manipulation — use template refs
- Never leave off the error handling block in Tier 2
- Never hardcode Salesforce record IDs in JS
- Never skip the lightning-spinner in Tier 2 components
- Never use innerHTML — it is not supported in LWC
- Never forget the key={uniqueField} attribute on for:each items
- Never generate a FlexiPage XML file — the pipeline handles page placement itself.
  When the ticket asks for the component to go on a record page, FlexiPageHelper appends it
  to the existing page in Apex, working from the page's real deployed XML. Emitting your own
  FlexiPage would overwrite that page and destroy every component already on it.
- Never generate a CSS file — out of scope for now
- Never use an uppercase letter anywhere in a CustomEvent name — new CustomEvent('contactSelect', ...)
  will silently fail to fire the parent's handler; all event names must be entirely lowercase.
- Never call this.dispatchEvent from a PARENT expecting a CHILD to receive it — CustomEvent from a
  child's own dispatchEvent always flows upward (child→parent) only.
- Never bind a child's @api property using the same casing in HTML as in JS — HTML custom attributes
  are kebab-case (contact-id={x}), JS class properties are camelCase (@api contactId).
- Never use this.template.querySelector to call a method or read a property on a CHILD custom element
  across a component boundary — use lwc:ref="name" on the child tag and this.refs.name in JS instead.
- Never subscribe() to a Lightning Message Channel without also unsubscribing in disconnectedCallback —
  this leaks a stale listener on every re-render/navigation cycle.
- Never call publish() or subscribe() without a wired @wire(MessageContext) messageContext in the same class.
- Never split a LightningMessageChannel into multiple file entries — it is always exactly one XML file,
  unlike the 3-file LwcHtml/LwcJs/LwcMeta bundle.
- Never invent a message channel field name that isn't declared in that channel's own
  <lightningMessageFields> — publish() payload keys and subscribe() handler reads must match the channel XML.
- Never reach for Lightning Message Service when a shared parent component already exists (or is being
  generated in the same ticket) — use the shared-parent pattern instead; LMS is for components with no
  shared ancestor.
- Never guess at an existing child component's @api contract when a multi-component ticket references a
  child not covered by orgState.existingLwcComponent — generate that child fresh in this response.
